     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Who Was Alfred Adler?",
     "content": "**Alfred Adler** (1870-1937) was an Austrian physician and psychotherapist — and one of the three giants of early 20th-century depth psychology, alongside **Sigmund Freud** and **Carl Jung**.\n\nAdler joined Freud's Vienna Psychoanalytic Society in 1902 and was for years its president. But by 1911, the cracks were obvious. Adler rejected Freud's emphasis on sexuality and unconscious drives. He broke away and founded what he called **Individual Psychology** (Individualpsychologie) — 'individual' meaning *indivisible*, the whole person.\n\nKey biographical facts:\n• Trained as an ophthalmologist before turning to psychiatry\n• Grew up sickly and felt overshadowed by an older brother — direct fuel for his theory of **inferiority feelings**\n• Fled Nazi Austria; lectured widely in the US\n• Died of a heart attack on a lecture tour in Aberdeen, Scotland, in 1937\n\nIn the English-speaking world, Adler's name faded. But his fingerprints are everywhere: in **cognitive behavioral therapy**, in **humanistic psychology** (Maslow called Adler 'more correct than Freud'), in **Stephen Covey's seven habits**, in modern coaching. The book you are about to dive into is the Japanese rediscovery of a thinker the West nearly forgot.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" class=\"lesson-visual\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#ffd700\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"36\">ALFRED ADLER (1870-1937)</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"24\">The third giant of depth psychology</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"180\" width=\"940\" height=\"160\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(99,102,241,0.12)\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"235\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6366f1\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">VIENNA, 1902-1911</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Member, then PRESIDENT of Freud's Psychoanalytic Society</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"315\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">By 1911 he broke away — rejected Freud's view of sexuality</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"380\" width=\"940\" height=\"160\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(139,92,246,0.12)\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"435\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">INDIVIDUAL PSYCHOLOGY</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"480\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">\"Individual\" = indivisible. The WHOLE person.</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"515\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">A unified self, not a battle of drives and ego</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"580\" width=\"940\" height=\"160\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(16,185,129,0.12)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"635\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">HIS LEGACY HIDES IN PLAIN SIGHT</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"680\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">CBT • Humanistic Psychology • Coaching • Covey's 7 Habits</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"715\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">Maslow: \"Adler was more and more correct year by year.\"</text><rect x=\"80\" y=\"780\" width=\"940\" height=\"160\" rx=\"20\" fill=\"rgba(245,158,11,0.12)\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"835\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"26\">JAPANESE REDISCOVERY</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"880\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"22\">Ichiro Kishimi spent 30 years studying Adler in Kyoto</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"915\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#888\" font-size=\"20\">The 2013 book brought Adler back to the world stage</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Adler: lost giant, recovered"
     }
