     "type": "application",
     "title": "Build the Tripod This Week",
     "content": "Three days, three pillars. Do one each day.\n\n**Day 1 — Self-acceptance.** Pick the single feature of your life you most resist. Your body shape. Your salary. Your family of origin. Your age. Your singleness. Your introversion. Whatever it is. For one full day, every time you catch yourself in the resistance — the bitter inner sentence, the comparison, the spiral — **stop and name it as a fact**. *'This is what is.'* Don't praise it. Don't curse it. Just *let it be true*. Notice that something subtle relaxes when you stop fighting the unchangeable.\n\n**Day 2 — Trust.** Choose one interaction today and *deliberately extend trust* you would normally withhold. Lend a book without verifying the borrower's track record. Tell a colleague something real without first calculating whether they'll use it against you. Ask a question that reveals you don't already know the answer. *Notice what happens.* In most cases — much more than you expect — the trust lands. The person rises to it. And in the few cases it doesn't, you discover quickly, with little real cost.\n\n**Day 3 — Contribution.** Make one act of contribution today that nobody will know about. Anonymous, unmarked, unrewarded. Refile a neighbor's misplaced mail. Pick up trash on a stranger's lawn. Send money to a cause that won't email you back. **Notice the texture of the worth** that arises in you when no audience can compound it. That is the steady worth. That is the *floor*.\n\nAt the end of the three days, sit for five minutes and ask: *which pillar was hardest? Which was easiest? Which one is missing most in my actual life?* Whatever you answer, that is your next month's curriculum.",
