     "type": "intro",
     "title": "The Most Misread Idea in the Book",
     "content": "Separation of tasks, the principle, covered earlier in this book, that you should not interfere in tasks whose consequences belong to someone else, and should not let others interfere in tasks whose consequences belong to you, is arguably the single most quoted, and most frequently misread, idea to come out of The Courage to Be Disliked. Stripped of context and reduced to a slogan, it circulates as something close to not my problem: a parent citing it to justify disengaging from a struggling child's emotional life, an employee citing it to refuse all feedback from a manager, a friend citing it to stop checking in on someone going through a hard time, all under the banner of healthy boundaries. This lesson takes seriously the question of whether that popular reading is a fair extension of the concept, an unfair strawman built from selective quotation, or something in between, a real risk the concept invites even though it is not what the concept, read in full, actually claims. The stakes are not merely academic. If separation of tasks really does function as a permission slip for withdrawal once stripped of its surrounding argument about horizontal relationships and community feeling, that is worth knowing before recommending the book, or the phrase, to someone navigating a genuinely dependent relationship, a young child, an aging parent, a friend in crisis, where showing up may matter more than staying out.",
