     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Steelmanning the Critique, Then the Reply",
     "content": "Give the critique its full force before answering it. Taken to an extreme, separation of tasks could plausibly rationalize a parent who stops engaging with a struggling child's emotional distress on the grounds that the child's inner life is the child's own task, an employee who refuses every piece of feedback from a manager by insisting your judgment of my work is your task, not mine, or a general pattern of social withdrawal relabeled, after the fact, as healthy boundary-setting rather than avoidance. None of these are wildly implausible misuses, each simply takes the principle at face value and drops the surrounding argument that is supposed to constrain it. The Adlerian reply, read in full rather than in slogan form, is that separation of tasks was never intended to end relationship, only to end interference and control as the mode within a relationship. The book pairs the concept explicitly with horizontal relationships, meaning relating to others as equals rather than from above or below, and with offering encouragement or support when it is wanted rather than either withholding it or imposing it uninvited. Under this fuller reading, whose task is this is a question about who owns the decision and who bears its consequences, not a question about who is permitted to care. A parent can decline to make a teenager's choices for them while remaining fully available, attentive, and responsive, task separation restricts control, not presence. That said, a genuine gap remains, and steelmanning the critique means admitting it rather than explaining it away: the book gives limited concrete guidance for situations involving real power asymmetry, young children, dependents, people in acute crisis, where waiting passively to be asked for help can itself cause serious harm, and restating horizontal relationships does not fully resolve that gap on its own.",
